{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/marin-community/marin/pulls/9708","id":4722732394,"node_id":"PR_kwDOLj5faM8AAAABGX8tag","html_url":"https://github.com/marin-community/marin/pull/9708","diff_url":"https://github.com/marin-community/marin/pull/9708.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/marin-community/marin/pull/9708.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/marin-community/marin/issues/9708","commits_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/marin-community/marin/pulls/9708/commits","review_comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/marin-community/marin/pulls/9708/comments","review_comment_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/marin-community/marin/pulls/comments{/number}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/marin-community/marin/issues/9708/comments","statuses_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/marin-community/marin/statuses/2b159517c1d266280729b87dc9ab71a7e77c96e3","number":9708,"state":"open","locked":false,"title":"[grug] Raise hero single-rack MFU from 28.2% to 31.2%","user":{"login":"mcwitt","id":319411,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMxOTQxMQ==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/319411?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mcwitt","html_url":"https://github.com/mcwitt","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mcwitt/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mcwitt/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mcwitt/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mcwitt/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mcwitt/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mcwitt/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mcwitt/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mcwitt/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mcwitt/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"On one GB200 NVL72 rack, these commits shorten the `moe_hero_ep` training step from 13.90 to 12.59 s (28.24% to 31.18% MFU), with loss inside the run-to-run noise of unchanged code. The PGLE profile builder adds no change by default; with a profile built from the program's own trace, the tip before the last two commits ran 12.515 s/step (31.37% MFU, +0.77% over 12.611 without a profile). The changes come from profiling the step's most expensive scopes and from benchmarking OLMo-core 3's BF16 MoE kernels against this path (#9636).\n\nEach row is one run on one rack (`cw-us-east-08a`, 64 GPUs), restored from `hero-fa4sm100-nomask-step146k` step 180000 with seed 0 for 100 steps, on main's production PJRT wheel. The time is the median step over steps 180011-180099 without the three profiled steps; the gain is the previous row's time divided by this row's, minus one. Repeated runs of the same code differ by about 0.1%. The runs predate the final refactors and review fixes, and the hero's train step lowers to identical StableHLO before and after them.\n\n| Change | Commit | s/step | MFU | Gain |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| main | b4d1761ff2 | 13.899 | 28.24% | |\n| Remat discounts the host-offloaded carry | 13448300c6 | 13.737 | 28.57% | +1.18% |\n| Unfilled ragged transport buffers | a544f2571c | 13.542 | 28.99% | +1.44% |\n| Expert-side routing-weight gradient | 9383ef90cc | 13.130 | 29.90% | +3.14% |\n| Streaming Triton short convolution | 5600e67b2e | 13.059 | 30.06% | +0.55% |\n| SwiGLU backward in the dh GEMM epilogue | c573fecfe0 | 12.943 | 30.33% | +0.89% |\n| Device-side kernel availability check | 99d961ed43 | | | no hero change |\n| Triton router top-k | 7d28781d70 | 12.875 | 30.49% | +0.53% |\n| Token-major dispatch scatter | 3ebb6f91b1 | 12.730 | 30.83% | +1.14% |\n| Shared and chunk GEMMs under the MoE transports | a225408148 | 12.638 | 31.06% | +0.73% |\n| Synchronous collectives without the carry offload | 7caae09edc | | | no hero change |\n| Shared-expert SwiGLU in QuACK epilogues | b24cfe37f8 | 12.652 | 31.03% | -0.11% |\n| No routed pre-activation store under remat | 332b1f0b02 | 12.611 | 31.13% | +0.33% |\n| PGLE profile builder | 379fdf0e66 | | | no hero change by default |\n| End-to-end test that no transport consumer reads unwritten rows | ea02772298 | | | no hero change |\n| Short conv and router top-k in Pallas instead of raw Triton | 729a0757f4, fea1451269 | 12.591 | 31.18% | +0.15% |\n| Packed bf16 short-conv rounding only on SM90 and newer | 088095b07f | | | no hero change |\n| Poisoning test retraces its recompute for each fill | 2b159517c1 | | | no hero change |\n\nThe scheduling row bundles three changes that do not stand alone. The MLP-section weight prefetch (from #9481) lets the first shared expert's gate/up projection run beside the first ragged dispatch, but it puts a weight slice behind each layer's carry-offload copy on the same memcpy stream, which stalls the forward by 98-152 ms/step. A barrier that issues the slices before the copy removes the stall; disabling it at the tip slows the step by 0.79%. The shared-expert QuACK row is negative because the scheduling row relies on the shared GEMMs to cover the transports: the 0.18 s/step of compute it saves exposes 0.24 s/step of transport. Removing it from the tip still slows the step, by 0.35% and 0.22% in two pairs.\n\nThe last row's gain is against a fresh run of the previous tip, 12.610 s/step, made beside it. Its kernels compile through the same Triton compiler: per call on GB200 the short convolution's forward and backward take 230 and 404 us against 244 and 449 us at 6,144 channels, and the top-k 0.107 against 0.127 ms. Pallas also lets the kernels' tests run on CPU through its interpreter.\n\nEach change that gained less than about 0.3% was removed from the tip and kept only if the tip then slowed by more than 0.2%, with a second pair for results between 0.2% and 0.4%. This dropped a scatter-based inverse permutation (the tip was 0.04% faster without it) and a Triton bincount (0.22% and 0.10%), and kept the pre-activation change (0.47%). The rows from the unfilled transport buffers through the dh-epilogue SwiGLU backward were measured with the inverse permutation in their base; without it, the last of them ran the same 12.943 s.\n\nThe unfilled transport buffers, top-k kernel, dispatch scatter and pre-activation change are bitwise, as is the Pallas top-k; the Pallas short convolution keeps its forward and `dx` bitwise, with its weight gradient within fp32 summation-order rounding. The routing-weight gradient, the dh-epilogue SwiGLU backward and the short convolution's weight gradient change at bf16 rounding. The shared-expert SwiGLU now runs on the fp32 accumulator, which changes 0.06-0.3% of downstream routing assignments and stays as close to an fp32 reference as before; the carry barrier moves fusion boundaries. Over 100 steps the tip's loss differs from main by at most 3.8e-4 (2.6e-4 with the profile), against 3.3e-4 between two runs of main.\n\n`moe_mlp` and `MoEExpertMlp` gain `routing_weight_gradient`. The default, `EXACT`, keeps the expert outputs for the backward. `EXPERT_SIDE` takes the gradient from the expert MLP's own backward and skips storing and returning the outputs, but the gradient is zero or inexact wherever `w * dout` rounds to zero in the cotangent dtype, as at a zero weight or a small float16 product. The hero selects `EXPERT_SIDE` (positive sigmoid weights, bf16 cotangents); other ragged users keep `EXACT`.\n\nPeak HBM rises from 103.1 to 123.2 GiB of a 143.75 GiB pool, mainly the 18 GiB routed MoE output the hero saves for the backward; under the carry offload the launcher defaults to `XLA_PYTHON_CLIENT_MEM_FRACTION=0.78` and slop 105, validated on one rack. A ragged run without the carry offload keeps XLA's default scheduler, which ignores the one-collective limit, so it now runs collectives synchronously; the hero always offloads. A PGLE profile matches one program and mesh, so the production run needs its own before it gains the profile's 0.77%; automatic PGLE stays off. Giving host transfers their own memcpy streams in the PJRT wheel would remove the copy collision for any program; that is left for a follow-up.\n\nThis supersedes #9481. It carries that PR's mirror transpose parameters, weight prefetch and PGLE builder, and replaces its two-stage chunk pipelining with the dispatch overlap above, which with PGLE measured 12.666 s/step against 12.783 for pipelining with PGLE on a PJRT wheel without the copy stall, both before the kernels above. Part of #9636; the OLMo-core comparison is on that issue, and the research records are `.agents/logbooks/mfu30.md` and `.agents/logbooks/olmo9636.md` on `research/mcwitt/mfu30`.\n","labels":[{"id":10166074947,"node_id":"LA_kwDOLj5faM8AAAACXfH-Qw","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/marin-community/marin/labels/agent-generated","name":"agent-generated","description":"Created by automation/agent","color":"1D76DB","default":false,"archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":11981276996,"node_id":"LA_kwDOLj5faM8AAAACyiPHRA","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/marin-community/marin/labels/agentic-lint","name":"agentic-lint","description":"Local agentic lint review completed","color":"1D76DB","default":false,"archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"milestone":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"created_at":"2026-10-03T00:02:54Z","updated_at":"2026-10-06T19:08:59Z","closed_at":null,"merged_at":null,"merge_commit_sha":"9dfcda3bea8c76eb64de7f4a0bd6262f83b0f9c2","assignee":null,"assignees":[],"requested_reviewers":[{"login":"dlwh","id":9633,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjk2MzM=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/9633?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dlwh","html_url":"https://github.com/dlwh","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dlwh/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dlwh/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dlwh/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dlwh/starr